CAT = "cisSpecialty"; SEG = "SPC"
Q = [
("Under FDA's device classification, what is a manual rongeur used for?",
 'Biting bone of the skull or spine', ['Holding a curved suture needle', 'Retracting the abdominal wall', 'Clamping a bleeding vessel'],
 '21 CFR 882.4840 describes a manual rongeur as a manually operated instrument for cutting or biting bone during surgery on the skull or spinal column.'),
('How does an osteotome differ from a bone chisel?',
 'It is beveled on both sides', ['It has a hollow, cannulated tip', 'It is beveled on one side only', 'It is driven by a power motor'],
 'An osteotome tapers to an edge from both sides, while a chisel is beveled on one side only. Both are struck with a mallet to cut or shape bone.'),
('Which orthopedic instrument has a cup-shaped tip used to scrape bone or tissue?',
 'Curette', ['Rasp', 'Broach', 'Bone tamp'],
 'A curette has a sharp-edged cup or loop for scraping. A rasp files bone with rows of teeth, and a tamp packs bone graft or seats an implant.'),
('During a hip replacement, which instrument shapes the femoral canal to fit the stem?',
 'Broach', ['Curette', 'Bone hook', 'Mallet'],
 'A broach is a toothed, stem-shaped instrument driven into the femoral canal to shape it for the implant. A bone hook is used to lift or pull bone.'),
('Which instrument is used to enlarge and shape a bony cavity, such as the acetabulum, with a rotating cutter?',
 'Reamer', ['Rongeur', 'Osteotome', 'Tenaculum'],
 "A reamer has cutting flutes and is turned by hand or power to enlarge a canal or cup. FDA's orthopedic manual instrument list includes reamers and rongeurs."),
('A Cobb instrument in a spine set is used to strip soft tissue off bone. What type of instrument is it?',
 'Periosteal elevator', ['Self-retaining retractor', 'Bone-cutting forceps', 'Suction tip'],
 'A Cobb is a periosteal elevator with a broad, flat blade for lifting periosteum and muscle off bone during spine surgery.'),
('A Penfield instrument in a neurosurgery set has a smooth, blunt blade. What is it used for?',
 'Dissecting delicate tissue', ['Cutting through the skull', 'Clamping large vessels shut', 'Holding skin edges open'],
 'Penfield dissectors are numbered blunt dissectors used to separate delicate tissue such as dura from bone. Cutting through skull uses a craniotome or perforator.'),
('What is a Gelpi retractor?',
 'A self-retaining, pointed retractor', ['A handheld retractor with a flat blade', 'A curved, angled suction tip', 'A spring-handled needle holder'],
 'A Gelpi has two pointed tips held apart by a ratchet, so it stays open without being held. It is common in spine and orthopedic sets.'),
('Why do bayonet forceps have an offset, angled shaft?',
 'So the hand does not block the view', ['So they can hold heavier tissue', 'So they lock with a ratchet', 'So they fit onto a power handle'],
 "The bayonet bend keeps the surgeon's hand out of the line of sight when working down a narrow opening, as in nasal or neurosurgery."),
('A suction tip has a small thumb port near the handle. What does covering the port do?',
 'It turns suction on at the tip', ['It locks the tip in place', 'It opens a second channel', 'It cuts off the light source'],
 'On suction tips such as the Frazier, covering the thumb port directs suction to the tip; uncovering it lets air in and weakens the suction.'),
('A spring-handled needle holder with fine jaws is most likely used in which specialty?',
 'Ophthalmic or microsurgery', ['Orthopedic trauma surgery', 'Abdominal general surgery', 'Dental extraction'],
 'Spring-handled holders such as the Castroviejo are made for fine sutures in eye and microsurgery. Ring-handled holders are used for larger needles.'),
("FDA's list of manual ophthalmic surgical instruments includes the eyelid speculum. What does a lid speculum do?",
 'Holds the eyelids open', ['Cuts the anterior lens capsule', "Measures the eye's pressure", 'Removes the cornea'],
 'A lid speculum keeps the eyelids apart for the length of eye surgery. FDA lists ophthalmic specula under 21 CFR 886.4350.'),
('In cataract surgery, what is a cystotome used for?',
 'Opening the lens capsule', ['Holding the iris back', 'Suturing the corneal incision', 'Measuring the incision width'],
 'A cystotome is a fine cutting needle or hook used to start the opening in the anterior lens capsule. FDA lists it among manual ophthalmic instruments.'),
("FDA's ENT instrument list describes a tracheal bistoury. What is it?",
 'A long, narrow knife', ['A tracheal suction catheter', 'A mouth gag with a ratchet', 'A nasal speculum'],
 '21 CFR 874.4420 defines the tracheal bistoury as a long, narrow surgical knife. The same list includes adenotomes, tonsil snares and ear curettes.'),
('Which ENT instrument holds the mouth open during a tonsillectomy?',
 'Mouth gag', ['Tonsil snare', 'Ear curette', 'Nasal rasp'],
 'A mouth gag keeps the jaws apart and the tongue down for tonsil and adenoid surgery. A tonsil snare is used to cut the tonsil free.'),
("What does an amniotome do, according to FDA's classification of obstetric instruments?",
 'It ruptures the membranes', ['It clamps the umbilical cord', 'It scrapes the uterine lining', 'It dilates the cervix'],
 '21 CFR 884.4530 defines an amniotome as an instrument used to rupture the fetal membranes. An umbilical clamp compresses the cord.'),
('A set of graduated metal rods used one after another to open the cervix is which instrument?',
 'Cervical dilators', ['Uterine sounds', 'Graves vaginal specula', 'Uterine curettes'],
 'FDA describes fixed-size cervical dilators as a series of bougies of various sizes used to dilate the cervical os by stretching the cervix.'),
('What is a tenaculum used for in gynecologic surgery?',
 'Grasping the cervix', ['Measuring uterine depth', 'Cutting a cervical cone', 'Holding the vagina open'],
 'A tenaculum has sharp hooked tips that grasp the cervix to hold and steady it. A uterine sound measures the depth of the uterus.'),
('Which clamp is side-biting, so it partly closes a large vessel while blood still flows past?',
 'Satinsky', ['Kocher', 'Allis', 'Mosquito hemostat'],
 'A Satinsky clamp is curved to clamp part of a vessel wall, such as the aorta, without stopping all flow. Kocher and Allis clamps have teeth for tissue.'),
('Why are DeBakey forceps used on blood vessels?',
 'Fine serrations are atraumatic', ['Large teeth grip tissue firmly', 'Their tips are insulated', 'Their jaws lock shut with a ratchet'],
 'DeBakey forceps have rows of fine, interlocking serrations that hold vessel walls without crushing them. Toothed forceps would tear the vessel.'),
('Small spring clamps that occlude a vessel temporarily during vascular surgery are called what?',
 'Bulldog clamps', ['Towel clips', 'Implanted hemoclips', 'Sponge ring forceps'],
 'Bulldog clamps are small, spring-loaded clamps that stop flow in a vessel for a short time without a handle on the field. Hemoclips are implanted and stay in.'),
('Which scissors have angled blades and are used to open an artery lengthwise?',
 'Potts scissors', ['Mayo scissors', 'Bandage scissors', 'Suture scissors'],
 'Potts scissors have fine, angled blades that extend an arteriotomy. Mayo scissors are heavy scissors for tougher tissue and sutures.'),
('Which heavy scissors are used to cut sutures and tough tissue such as fascia?',
 'Mayo scissors', ['Potts scissors', 'Iris scissors', 'Westcott scissors'],
 'Mayo scissors are sturdy and come straight or curved for tough tissue and sutures. Potts, iris and Westcott scissors are fine scissors for vessels and eye surgery.'),
('A broad hand-held retractor curved like a question mark, used in deep abdominal surgery, is which?',
 'Deaver', ['Gelpi', 'Weitlaner', 'Senn'],
 'The Deaver is a wide, smooth, curved hand-held retractor for deep abdominal and chest work. Gelpi and Weitlaner retractors are self-retaining.'),
('Which suffix means creating a surgical opening to the outside of the body, as in colostomy?',
 '-ostomy', ['-otomy', '-ectomy', '-plasty'],
 '-ostomy is an opening brought to the skin surface. -otomy is a cut into an organ, -ectomy a removal and -plasty a repair.'),
('A laminectomy removes part of which structure?',
 'A vertebra', ['The skull', 'A rib', 'The kneecap'],
 'The lamina is part of the arch at the back of a vertebra. Laminectomy sets carry Kerrison rongeurs and other instruments to remove bone around the spinal canal.'),
('What does arthroplasty mean?',
 'Repair or replacement of a joint', ['Viewing a joint with a scope', 'Removal of the joint capsule', 'Fusion of two vertebrae'],
 'Arthro- means joint and -plasty means repair or reconstruction, so arthroplasty includes total hip and knee replacement. Arthroscopy is the scope procedure.'),
('A craniotomy set is requested. What is the procedure?',
 'An opening in the skull', ['Removal of a kidney', 'Repair of an inguinal hernia', 'A look into the bladder'],
 'Crani- refers to the skull and -otomy to an incision or opening, so a craniotomy opens the skull to reach the brain.'),
('Why can magnetized microsurgical instruments be a problem?',
 'Fine needles cling to them', ['They rust faster in steam', 'They set off the washer alarm', 'They lose their laser markings'],
 'The outline lists magnetization of delicate instruments as a risk factor. A magnetized tip pulls tiny needles and makes fine work harder.'),
('Which instrument in a dental set carries amalgam to the tooth?',
 'Amalgam carrier', ['Burnisher', 'Periodontal probe', 'Root canal plugger'],
 '21 CFR 872.4565 lists the amalgam carrier with burnishers, carvers, probes and pluggers. A burnisher smooths a filling after it is placed.'),
('Which rongeur has a long shaft and a footplate that bites upward, and is used to remove bone around the spinal canal?',
 'Kerrison rongeur', ['Leksell rongeur', 'Stille bone cutter', 'Liston bone cutter'],
 "The Kerrison's thin footplate slips under the edge of the lamina and cuts upward toward the handle. The Leksell is a double-action rongeur with a cupped jaw for nibbling larger pieces of bone."),
('Which instrument is passed into the urethra to dilate it or find a stricture?',
 'Urethral sound', ['Uterine sound', 'Tonsil snare', 'Bone tamp'],
 'Urethral sounds, such as Van Buren sounds, are curved, graduated rods for dilating the urethra. A uterine sound measures the uterus.'),
]
